course: world
lesson: 193
title: Olympiad: logic traps and if-then ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Logic puzzles are built from small, safe moves: if A always brings B and there is no B, then there was no A. Most traps are moves that only look safe, like running an "if" backwards.
explain: To test a rule, look only where it could break. "If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other": a card showing A could break it, and so could a card showing 7, but a card showing 4 can't, whatever is on its back.
explain: Picture an island where some people always tell the truth and some always lie. Nobody there can ever say "I am a liar": a truth-teller would be lying and a liar would be telling the truth.
explain: Order puzzles need care too: "Ann is taller than Ben, and Cal is taller than Ben" tells you who is shortest, but not who is tallest.
explain: Words like "all", "some", "not all" and "only" carry the whole puzzle. The opposite of "all the kids came" is not "no kids came" but "at least one kid didn't come".
fact: If "if A, then B" is true and B did not happen, then A did not happen; this move is always safe.
fact: To check "if a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other" with cards showing A, K, 4 and 7, you must turn over A and 7, and no others.
fact: On an island of truth-tellers and liars, nobody can say "I am a liar"; if two people there say "the other one is a liar" and "we both tell the truth", the first is a truth-teller and the second a liar.
fact: "Ann is taller than Ben and Cal is taller than Ben" shows Ben is the shortest but doesn't say whether Ann or Cal is taller.
fact: The opposite of "all the kids came" is "at least one kid didn't come".
fact: "Some cats are black and some black things are bats" does not mean some cats are bats; the black cats and the black bats can be different animals.
fact: "Only people with tickets get in" means anyone who got in had a ticket; it doesn't promise that everyone with a ticket got in.
fact: One true example against an "all" claim, like a penguin against "all birds can fly", is enough to show it is false.
fact: A fair coin has a half chance of heads on every throw, even after 5 heads in a row; it has no memory.
fact: "It's not untrue" means "it's true": two "nots" cancel.
fact: When three boxes are labelled Apples, Oranges and Mixed and every label is wrong, taking one fruit from the box labelled Mixed is enough to put all three labels right.
fact: Ice cream sales and sunburn go up together because hot sunny days cause both, not because ice cream causes sunburn.
fact: Words people use: "sus" means suspicious; "mid" means just average, not good; "ken" (Scotland) means know; "gadgie" (North East England) means a man, a bloke; "yinz" (Pittsburgh, USA) means you all.
exercise: Cards show A, K, 4 and 7. Rule: "If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other." Which cards must you turn to check? | A and 7 | A and 4 | Only A | A, 4 and 7
exercise: The rule "If it's Tuesday, we have swimming" is always kept. Today there is no swimming. What follows? | Today is not Tuesday | Today is Tuesday | Today might be Tuesday | Today is the weekend
exercise: On the island of truth-tellers and liars, which of these can nobody ever say? | "I am a liar" | "I am a truth-teller" | "My friend is a liar" | "The sky is blue"
exercise: Ann is taller than Ben. Cal is taller than Ben. Who is the tallest? | You can't tell from this | Ann | Cal | Ben
exercise: Ann is taller than Ben, Ben is taller than Cal, and Dan is shorter than Cal. Who is the shortest? | Dan | Cal | Ben | Ann
exercise: What is the opposite of "All the kids came to the party"? | At least one kid didn't come | No kids came to the party at all | Some kids came to the party | All the kids stayed at home
exercise: "Some cats are black. Some black things are bats. So some cats are bats." What is wrong? | The black cats and black bats can be different animals | Nothing; the steps are right | No cats are ever black | Bats aren't black
exercise: "Only people with tickets get in." Jo got in. What must be true? | Jo had a ticket | Jo had no ticket | Jo bought two tickets | Nothing can be said
exercise: A fair coin has landed heads 5 times in a row. What is the chance of heads next time? | Still a half | Less than a half | More than a half | No chance
exercise: Max says, "It's not untrue that I ate the cake." What did he say? | He ate it | He didn't eat it | He ate half of it | Someone else ate it
exercise: Which one fact is enough to show that "all birds can fly" is false? | A penguin is a bird and can't fly | Most birds can fly a long way | Bats can fly but are not birds | Some birds fly south for the winter
exercise: Three boxes are labelled Apples, Oranges and Mixed, and every label is wrong. Which box should you take one fruit from to fix all the labels? | The box labelled Mixed | The box labelled Apples | The box labelled Oranges | You need to look in all three boxes
exercise: On the island, Ava says, "Ben is a liar." Ben says, "We both tell the truth." Who tells the truth? | Ava, and Ben lies | Ben, and Ava lies | Both of them | Neither of them
exercise: The pool sign: "If you're under 12, you wear armbands." Who must the lifeguard check? | A 10-year-old, and someone without armbands | Everyone wearing armbands in the pool | A 14-year-old, and a 10-year-old | Only the people without armbands
exercise: Ice cream sales and sunburn go up together. Why? | Hot sunny days cause both | Ice cream causes sunburn | Sunburn makes people want ice cream | It is pure luck
test: Ethan: "Mate, the rule's 'vowel means even on the back'. I flipped the 4 and there's a K. Rule's broken!" Is he right? | No; the rule says nothing about what's behind a 4 | Yes; a 4 must always have a vowel behind it | Yes; any card at all can break a rule | No; he should have flipped the K instead
test: Ruby from London: "There's no swimming today, bruv, so it's defo Tuesday, innit?" The rule "if Tuesday, then swimming" is always kept. What's true? | It is not Tuesday | It is Tuesday | It might be Tuesday | Every day is a swimming day
test: A man on the island grins: "I am a liar." Ellie: "Sus. That's impossible here." Why is she right? | A truth-teller can't say it and a liar can't either | Liars on the island are never allowed to talk | Only truth-tellers can say it, so he must be one | Only liars can say it, so he must be one of them
test: Jamie from Newcastle: "Howay, Ann's taller than Ben and Cal's taller than Ben, so Ann's the tallest, the gadgie said." What is true? | Only that Ben is the shortest | Ann is the tallest | Cal is the tallest | Ann and Cal are the same height
test: Dad: "Not all of you tidied up." Leo: "So none of us tidied, mid effort all round." What did Leo get wrong? | "Not all" means at least one didn't, not that nobody did | "Not all" means exactly half of them tidied | "Not all" means everyone tidied a bit | Nothing; "not all" means nobody did
test: Grandpa from Pittsburgh: "Yinz know some cats are black and some black things are bats, so some cats are bats." What's the best reply? | That doesn't follow; they can be different animals | He's right; it follows straight from the two facts given | Only black cats can be bats | Bats can be cats at night only
test: Mason: "I've got a ticket and they still didn't let me in! The sign said only ticket holders get in!" What did the sign promise? | Only that nobody without a ticket gets in | That everyone with a ticket gets in | That tickets are free | That ticket holders always go to the front
test: Lily: "Five heads in a row! Tails is SO due now, it's basically guaranteed." What is true? | Each throw is still a half chance of heads | Tails is now certain | Heads is less likely, since it's had its turn | Heads is more likely now, as the coin is on a roll
test: Mum: "So it's 'not untrue' that you ate the whole cake?" Max: "Technically I didn't say I ate it!" What did Max really admit? | That he ate it | That he didn't eat it | That he can't remember | That the cake was not true
test: Aisha from Glasgow: "Ken what, every bird can fly, no exceptions." Her friend points at a penguin at the zoo. How many such birds does the friend need? | One is enough | At least ten | A thousand | Every bird on Earth
test: Owen, at a fruit stall: "All three labels are wrong? Then I'll have to open all three boxes, obviously." What's the clever way? | Take one fruit from the box labelled Mixed | Open all three, since a wrong label tells you nothing | Take a fruit from the Apples box; that settles it | Shake each box and listen
exam: It is given that the proposition "if it is Tuesday, swimming takes place" holds, and that no swimming takes place today. Determine what may be concluded. | Today is not Tuesday | Today is Tuesday | Today may be Tuesday | Nothing may be concluded
exam: Of four cards showing A, K, 4 and 7, identify those which must be turned in order to test the rule that a vowel on one face implies an even number on the other. | A and 7 | A and 4 | A alone | A, 4 and 7
exam: State the proposition which is the negation of "all the children attended". | At least one child did not attend | No child attended | Some children attended | All the children were absent
exam: On an island whose inhabitants either always speak truly or always speak falsely, identify the statement which no inhabitant can make. | "I am a liar" | "I speak the truth" | "You are a liar" | "Snow is white"
